Here's TUnit's lifecycle at each scope:

## 1. Before/After Every Test in a Class

```csharp
public class OrderTests
{
    [Before(Test)]
    public async Task ResetDatabase(TestContext context)
    {
        await InMemoryDb.ResetAsync();
    }

    [After(Test)]
    public async Task Cleanup(TestContext context)
    {
        // runs after each test
    }

    [Test]
    public async Task Order_IsValid() { /* ... */ }
}
```

## 2. Once Before First / After Last Test in a Class

```csharp
public class OrderTests
{
    [Before(Class)]
    public static async Task OpenFixture(ClassHookContext context)
    {
        // runs once before any test in this class
    }

    [After(Class)]
    public static async Task DisposeFixture(ClassHookContext context)
    {
        // runs once after all tests in this class
    }

    [Test]
    public async Task Order_IsValid() { /* ... */ }
}
```

## 3. Once per Assembly (before/after all tests in the assembly)

```csharp
public class WireMockSetup
{
    [Before(Assembly)]
    public static async Task StartServer(AssemblyHookContext context)
    {
        // start WireMock
    }

    [After(Assembly)]
    public static async Task StopServer(AssemblyHookContext context)
    {
        // stop WireMock
    }
}
```

## 4. Once per Entire Test Session

```csharp
public class GlobalDatabaseSetup
{
    [Before(TestSession)]
    public static async Task ApplyMigrations(TestSessionContext context)
    {
        // run EF Core migrations
    }

    [After(TestSession)]
    public static async Task TearDown(TestSessionContext context)
    {
        // drop database
    }
}
```

---

**Key points:**

| Scope | Attribute | Method must be |
|-------|-----------|----------------|
| Per-test | `[Before(Test)]` / `[After(Test)]` | Instance |
| Per-class | `[Before(Class)]` / `[After(Class)]` | `static` |
| Per-assembly | `[Before(Assembly)]` / `[After(Assembly)]` | `static` |
| Per-session | `[Before(TestSession)]` / `[After(TestSession)]` | `static` |

There's also `[BeforeEvery(Test)]` / `[AfterEvery(Test)]` (and `Class`/`Assembly` variants) for **global cross-cutting hooks** that fire for every test across the entire run, regardless of which class they're in — handy for logging or diagnostics.
